0. Definitions

   "The Work" means everything in this repository that I made or own:
   code, markup, styles, scripts, docs, graphics, and data I created or
   arranged. Section 5 lists what is not part of it.

   "The Official Repository" means this repository at
   github.com/ag0nyy-ops, and only that copy, as I committed it.

   "Copy" means reproducing the Work or any part of it in any form,
   including:
   a. pasting, downloading or saving it into anything else;
   b. rewriting, paraphrasing, renaming or restructuring it while you
      have the Work open, or while working from notes or memory of its
      specific code;
   c. translating or porting it to another programming language or
      framework;
   d. giving it to an AI tool, code generator or other person to
      rewrite, summarise into code, or reproduce, and using the result;
   e. reproducing the sequence, structure and organization of its
      specific code. Standard project layouts that a framework or tool
      generates (for example Tauri, Cargo or Xcode templates) are not
      part of the Work.

   "Personal Use" means use by one individual, for themselves, that is
   not commercial and not done for or on behalf of any business,
   school, organization or client.

1. What you may do

   a. View, read and study the Work on GitHub.
   b. Clone or download the Work to your own device, and keep that
      copy, only to study it. You may change and build that copy
      to learn how it works, as Personal Use, as long as you never
      share, publish, host or run the changed version for anyone else.
   c. Download and run release builds I publish in the Official
      Repository, unchanged, as Personal Use.
   d. Use the general ideas and concepts you learn here in your own
      work, if you write your own code without doing anything listed
      under "Copy". Copyright protects my code, not the ideas behind
      it, and this license doesn't claim otherwise.
   e. Quote short excerpts (no more than 15 lines each, and no more
      than 50 lines from any one repository in total) for
      non-commercial commentary, teaching or review, crediting Jaymz
      (ag0nyy-ops) with a link to the Official Repository. A
      walkthrough or course covering a substantial part of the Work
      needs my permission.

2. What you may not do without my permission (section 7)

   a. Copy the Work, or any part of it, into anything outside your own
      study copy under 1b.
   b. Distribute, publish, host, deploy, sell, rent or sublicense the
      Work or any changed version of it, as source or as a build.
   c. Use the Work, any part of it, or any changed version, in any
      other project or for anything other than Personal Use.
   d. Use the Work, or any part of it, to train, fine-tune, evaluate
      or build any AI or machine-learning model or dataset, or add it
      to any such dataset.
   e. Remove or change this license or any copyright notice.

3. GitHub

   GitHub's Terms of Service give GitHub users certain rights in public
   repositories, such as viewing and forking them on GitHub. Those
   rights stand as GitHub's terms define them, and this license does
   not take them away. Beyond what those terms require, a fork is
   still covered by every term here. Turning on GitHub
   Pages for a fork, publishing builds or releases from a fork (with
   GitHub Actions or otherwise), creating a repository from this one
   as a template, and any similar GitHub feature that makes the Work
   or a changed version available to others all count as hosting or
   distributing under section 2b.

4. Parts you may copy

   A part of the Work may be copied only if it carries one of these
   markers, in the Official Repository, committed by me:

   a. A whole file: a comment containing
          SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
      (or another named license) within the file's first 5 lines.
      That file is then under that license.
   b. A block: code between a comment containing exactly
          COPY-OK BEGIN (MIT)
      and the next comment containing exactly
          COPY-OK END
      in the same file. That block is under the MIT License. A BEGIN
      with no matching END grants nothing.

   A marker only counts if it is an actual comment in source code. Text
   inside a string, a test fixture, sample data, a README or other
   documentation is not a marker. Markers added by anyone else,
   including in a fork or in a contribution I merged, do not count.

   Copying marked code gives you no rights in any unmarked code it
   calls or depends on. When you copy marked code, you must follow the
   license it names in full; for MIT that means including the
   copyright line above and the full MIT permission notice.

5. What this license does not cover

   a. Third-party code, fonts, assets and data, which keep their own
      licenses or terms, named in the README or next to those files.
   b. Facts, and public-domain data (for example US government data)
      as such. My own selection and arrangement of it is still covered.
   c. Anything the law lets you do without permission, such as fair
      use. This license claims no more than the law and your agreement
      to it allow.

6. Contributions

   If you send me a contribution (a pull request, patch, issue code
